,"ess":0,"yt":"dQsjAbZDx-4","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Pop de los setenta y power-pop","scene_id":189117666316696,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#power-pop","chapters":["Sección 4.2"],"pages":[[1,82,null]]},{"id":52482063900367,"artist":"Abba","artist_id":106127024745038,"title":"Dancing Queen","year":1977,"ess":0,"yt":"xFrGuyw1V8s","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Pop de los setenta y power-pop","scene_id":189117666316696,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#power-pop","chapters":["Sección 4.2"],"pages":[[1,82,null]]},{"id":238671581490630,"artist":"Abba","artist_id":106127024745038,"title":"Knowing Me Knowing You","year":1977,"ess":0,"yt":"iUrzicaiRLU","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Pop de los setenta y power-pop","scene_id":189117666316696,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#power-pop","chapters":["Sección 4.2"],"pages":[[1,82,null]]},{"id":41124042298514,"artist":"Abba","artist_id":106127024745038,"title":"Gimme Gimme Gimme","year":1979,"ess":0,"yt":"XEjLoHdbVeE","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Pop de los setenta y power-pop","scene_id":189117666316696,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#power-pop","chapters":["Sección 4.2"],"pages":[[1,82,null]]},{"id":163035799374875,"artist":"Abba","artist_id":106127024745038,"title":"Winner Takes It All","year":1980,"ess":0,"yt":"92cwKCU8Z5c","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Pop de los setenta y power-pop","scene_id":189117666316696,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#power-pop","chapters":["Sección 4.2"],"pages":[[1,82,null]]},{"id":39146573614023,"artist":"Abba","artist_id":106127024745038,"title":"Lay All Your Love On Me","year":1981,"ess":0,"yt":"ulZQTrV8QlQ","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Pop de los setenta y power-pop","scene_id":189117666316696,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#power-pop","chapters":["Sección 4.2"],"pages":[[1,82,null]]},{"id":54329848085191,"artist":"Abba","artist_id":106127024745038,"title":"Supertrouper","year":1981,"ess":0,"yt":"BshxCIjNEjY","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Pop de los setenta y power-pop","scene_id":189117666316696,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#power-pop","chapters":["Sección 4.2"],"pages":[[1,82,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":82,"text":"Power-pop 1972-73 Abba were the reigning champions of pop during the 1970s. They began with effervescent vocal harmonies coupled with catchy upbeat refrains, notably…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":82,"text":"…melodrama with Knowing Me Knowing You (1977) and Winner Takes It All (1980). Supertrouper (1981) returned them to their naive-pop glory, and, after Abba disbanded, their leaders and songwriters managed to top all of this with a superb exotic pastiche, Murray Head's One Night In Bangkok (1985).…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":199,"text":"…(1989) epitomized the fusion of hypnotic disco beats, catchy melodies, surreal arrangements, and Sixties-style naif enthusiasm. Mixing Byrds with Abba, and Hendrix with Petula Clark, and James Brown and the Mamas & Papas, songs such as I Wanna Be Adored, She Bangs The Drums and Made Of Stone…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":261,"text":"…an approach that was the musical equivalent of Andy Warhol's pop art but that mocked everybody from soul to glam. Sweden seemed to specialize in Abba-like melodies sung to the techno beat, whether in a clearly Abba-derived fashion, as Ace Of Base did with All That She Wants (1992) and Beautiful Life (1995), or in an ironic synth-pop style, as Aqua (1) did on…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Eurovision Victory (1974)","body":"ABBA won the 1974 Eurovision Contest with their song \"Waterloo.\" \"and they were the winner of the 1974 Eurovision contest.\"","year":1974,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Second Appearance on Dina (1976)","body":"ABBA made their second appearance on Dina Shore's talk show on November 26, 1976, where they performed two songs: \"Fernando\" and \"Mamma Mia.\" \"On their second appearance on Dina, on that November 26, 1976 episode, Abba performed two songs, Fernando, and the song that you that very well may have saved their pop music careers, Mamma Mia.\"","year":1976,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"US Market Strategy and Live Presence (1976)","body":"In October 1976, while in Los Angeles for TV tapings, Benny Anderson stated that ABBA did not need the U.S. market, being already huge in Europe and Australia. He described their efforts to make it in the U.S. as \"more for the sport and the challenge,\" emphasizing that they had no interest in being an opening act and had virtually no live music presence in the United States, aside from a few cities on their 1976 world tour, with Seattle being the first. \"Benny Anderson made it clear that Abba did not need the United States. They were already huge in Europe and Australia by that time. They had no interest in being an opening act because it was not worth the time and the money and they didn't have a big enough audience in the United States to justify it.\"","year":1976,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Band Dissolution Due to Divorce (December 1982)","body":"Abba was uniquely composed of two married couples. When both of these couples eventually divorced, the band subsequently ended their operations in December 1982. \"Abba was made up of two couples and then both couples divorced, which ended the band in December 1982.\"","year":1982,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Couples in Music","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5Dhh5_PDhd0"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"John McCain's Fandom (2008)","body":"In 2008, during his presidential campaign, then-Arizona Senator John McCain made headlines for admitting to being a fan of Abba. He defended his preference by rhetorically asking, \"if they are so bad, why are they so popular?\" highlighting the connection between popularity and perceived value. \"And even Arizona Senator John McCain was taken to task for being a fan when he admitted it back in 2008 when he was running for president. He made headlines for defending Abba by saying, if they are so bad, why are they so popular?\"","year":2008,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"\"Mamma Mia\" as Career Catalyst","body":"The release of \"Mamma Mia,\" a song they were initially hesitant to release due to its \"too European\" title and even offered to Brotherhood of Man, ultimately saved ABBA from becoming one-hit wonders and launched their career in Europe and Australia. \"What saved them from the one hit wonder fate was a single that they were not even sure that they wanted to release Mama Mia.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"ABBA Voyage Residency","body":"In London, ABBA launched \"ABBA Voyage,\" a residency in a purpose-built theater that transports audiences back to the band's peak in the late 1970s and early 1980s. Uniquely, all members of ABBA are still alive but have no interest in performing live. The show utilizes motion capture suits and sophisticated projection technology to create lifelike \"Abbatars\" on stage. \"All the members of the band are still alive but have no interest in performing.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The 100 Greatest Rock Moments of the Millennium So Far - Part 1: (100-91)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GYUuTbOvyak"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Composition of the Group","body":"ABBA was a Swedish pop group composed of two married couples who later divorced. \"And the Swedish pop group that was comprised of what would be too married and then later two divorced couples.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"First European Artist in Top Sellers","body":"ABBA, the Swedish group, is noted as the first European artist to appear on the list of historically top-selling artists, with estimated sales around 60 million copies. This places them considerably lower than the dominant American and British acts.","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Music Radar Clan","episode":"Qué es un disco de ORO?    cómo se concede?","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GHVIyfsZBe4"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Identity Crisis and Emulation of Sweet","body":"Early in their career, ABBA experienced an identity crisis and considered emulating the glam band Sweet, who had hits like \"Fox on the Run,\" \"Little Willy,\" and \"Love Is Like Oxygen.\" Bjorn Olvea later stated this was \"stupid because we were always a pop group.\" \"They were trying to emulate sweet the glam band that had hits with like a fox on the run little Willie love is like oxygen.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Late Scopatone Films","body":"ABBA was among the artists who continued to make films for Scopatone machines even as their popularity declined after 1967. The last Scopatone film was made in 1978, highlighting the machine's prolonged but tapering existence. \"The Carpenters and Abba made films for the machines.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Rise and Fall and Future of the Music Video - Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lqd6IbUJ7tg"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Outrageous Stage Costumes Designed as Tax Deductions Under Swedish Law","body":"ABBA's deliberately extravagant and unwearable stage outfits from the 1970s were intentionally designed to be so unusual that they could not be used in everyday life, thereby qualifying as a tax deduction under Swedish law. \"Their unusual costumes were designed to be so extravagant that they couldn't be used in daily life, allowing a tax deduction under Swedish law.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"60 Mind-Blowing Facts About Music in 40 or so Minutes (2025 Edition)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0DFmS6MPbFI"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Ownership of Polar Studios","body":"The legendary Polar Studios in Stockholm was owned by the Swedish pop group ABBA. This studio gained further fame as the recording location for Led Zeppelin's \"In Through the Out Door\" and served as the architectural and functional template for Dave Grohl's 606 Studios. \"The template is a place called Polar Studios in Stockholm, which is owned by ABBA.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Ultra-Deep Background on Dave Grohl: Part 3","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PHLwjD-L724"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Revolutionizing Pop Music","body":"According to Frida, ABBA's emergence marked a significant shift in the music scene, suggesting that their unique style of pop music did not exist before them. \"Frida said at one point the music scene changed with us something like Abba did not exist before pop like that was not invented yet.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Rock and Roll Hall of Fame Inductees","body":"Abba has been inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame, a testament to their significant impact on music. \"Rock and roll, Hall of Famers, Abba.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Sampled by The Jams","body":"The Justified Ancients of Mu Mu (The Jams), a precursor to The KLF, used unauthorized samples from Abba in their material, leading to legal problems and an unsuccessful negotiation trip to Sweden. \"He immediately started causing problems by using unauthorized samples in their material, including from the Beals and Abba, which created all kinds of legal problems, which led them to travel to Sweden to try to negotiate with Abba.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Alt-Rock's Most Mysterious Musicians","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oGscVtrdER0"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Struggling After Eurovision Victory","body":"After winning Eurovision with \"Waterloo,\" ABBA faced difficulties with their follow-up singles in Europe, placing them on the verge of becoming a one-hit wonder before they had even gained recognition in the United States. \"The problem for Abba in Europe was that their follow up singles after they won Eurovision with Waterloo didn't really do very much. They were on the verge of becoming the dreaded one hit wonders before anyone in the United States had really ever even heard of them.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"US Popularity through Television and Radio","body":"ABBA's popularity in the United States was primarily driven by television and radio exposure, as they had minimal live presence in the country. Most Americans first saw them on TV shows like \"Saturday Night Live,\" \"American Bandstand\" in 1975, or their initial appearance on Dina Shore's show that same year. \"Abba's popularity in the United States stems from television and radio. They had virtually no live music presence in the United States ever.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Unique Mid-70s Pop Vibe","body":"ABBA possessed a distinct European look and sound, setting them apart from other mid-70s pop groups. They were not categorized as rock or part of the singer-songwriter movement, and their rise occurred before the full advent of the disco era in 1974. \"Abba had a different vibe from other pop groups of the mid 70s they were just European in their look in their sound they were not rock they were not part of the singer songwriter movement and we were not quite yet into the disco era in 1974.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 56 - Talk Shows of the 1970s","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"First prize of the Eurovision Song Contest (1974)","body":"Won.","year":1974,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q101421673"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Brit Award for International Album (1977) — Arrival","body":"Nominated.","year":1977,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q60774799"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Rockbjörnen for the century's Swedish group/artist (1999)","body":"Won.","year":1999,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q116373680"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Rock and Roll Hall of Fame (2010)","body":"Won.","year":2010,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q179191"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Musikexportpriset (2021)","body":"Won.","year":2021,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.